1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,511
Picture the moment right before this section existed.

2
00:00:03,511 --> 00:00:08,282
A script lands on Dana's desk claiming the socks are clinically proven to reduce

3
00:00:08,282 --> 00:00:09,832
swelling by forty percent.

4
00:00:09,832 --> 00:00:11,681
Nobody had ever run that study.

5
00:00:11,681 --> 00:00:15,796
If nobody had asked the right question, it ships under Harlan's name.

6
00:00:15,796 --> 00:00:20,388
That near miss was one specific kind of failure: a claim nobody could defend.

7
00:00:20,388 --> 00:00:25,397
It's one of exactly four ways an AI ad actually gets pulled, refunded, or flagged, a

8
00:00:25,397 --> 00:00:30,109
claim that overstates what the product does, a rights or licence gap behind the

9
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:35,118
person or the music, a brand or product that drifted from what it actually is, and a

10
00:00:35,118 --> 00:00:38,458
disclosure that's missing where a platform requires one.

11
00:00:38,458 --> 00:00:42,215
Nearly every real failure sorts cleanly into one of these four.

12
00:00:42,215 --> 00:00:47,224
Naming all four now, not just the one that caught Harlan, is what lets you check for

13
00:00:47,224 --> 00:00:49,729
a failure you haven't personally seen yet.

14
00:00:49,729 --> 00:00:54,679
You already know what a mangled spec overlay looks like, because Section 3.4 showed

15
00:00:54,679 --> 00:00:55,156
you one.

16
00:00:55,156 --> 00:00:58,674
You may never have seen a music-licensing gap specifically.

17
00:00:58,674 --> 00:01:03,505
But if you know "rights gap" is a category worth checking, you catch it the first

18
00:01:03,505 --> 00:01:08,157
time it shows up in your own batch, not after it's already cost you something.

19
00:01:08,707 --> 00:01:11,907
Notice this table isn't introducing four new rules.

20
00:01:11,907 --> 00:01:15,546
It's naming the pattern connecting rules you already have.

21
00:01:15,546 --> 00:01:18,872
Product drift is Section 3.3's brand-control problem.

22
00:01:18,872 --> 00:01:21,947
A rights gap is Section 4.1's licence-tier catch.

23
00:01:21,947 --> 00:01:25,649
You've been fixing individual instances of these all along.

24
00:01:25,649 --> 00:01:30,794
This section just gives you the category, so you can check for the pattern instead

25
00:01:30,794 --> 00:01:34,120
of waiting to recognise each specific instance again.

26
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:38,011
That's the actual value of a taxonomy over a list of examples.

27
00:01:38,011 --> 00:01:40,897
A list tells you what already went wrong once.

28
00:01:40,897 --> 00:01:45,729
A category tells you what to look for in a draft that's never failed this way

29
00:01:45,729 --> 00:01:46,168
before.

30
00:01:46,168 --> 00:01:51,188
Notice, too, that all four categories share one property: none of them are about

31
00:01:51,188 --> 00:01:52,631
whether the ad is good.

32
00:01:52,631 --> 00:01:57,777
A perfectly persuasive ad can still fail every one of these checks, and a mediocre

33
00:01:57,777 --> 00:01:59,659
one can pass all four cleanly.

34
00:01:59,659 --> 00:02:02,044
Section 8 handles whether an ad works.

35
00:02:02,044 --> 00:02:07,001
This section only asks whether it's allowed to run at all, a genuinely separate

36
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:10,327
question that's easy to blur under deadline pressure.

37
00:02:10,877 --> 00:02:15,792
Four questions, asked of every single draft, not four separate audits run once a

38
00:02:15,792 --> 00:02:16,284
quarter.

39
00:02:16,284 --> 00:02:21,445
That's the actual shape of the checklist Section 6.6 builds properly, and it's worth

40
00:02:21,445 --> 00:02:25,377
previewing here so you recognise it immediately when it arrives.

41
00:02:25,377 --> 00:02:27,651
Run them in this order, deliberately.

42
00:02:27,651 --> 00:02:30,600
Claims first, cheapest to fix, a line rewritten.

43
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:34,963
Rights second, can force a whole recast but still fixable before spend.

44
00:02:34,963 --> 00:02:37,912
Accuracy third, usually just needs regenerating.

45
00:02:37,912 --> 00:02:42,705
Disclosure last, almost never a reason to kill a draft, only a reason to add a

46
00:02:42,705 --> 00:02:43,073
label.

47
00:02:43,073 --> 00:02:46,944
Front-load the checks that catch the biggest problems earliest.

48
00:02:46,944 --> 00:02:50,078
Notice the questions are all answerable, not vague.

49
00:02:50,078 --> 00:02:55,178
"Could I defend this" has a yes or no. "Is every voice licensed" has a yes or no. A

50
00:02:55,178 --> 00:03:00,093
pre-flight check that asks vague questions gets skipped under deadline pressure.

51
00:03:00,093 --> 00:03:03,350
One that asks answerable questions gets actually run.

52
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:08,388
Which of these four would you be most tempted to skip on a Friday afternoon, batch

53
00:03:08,388 --> 00:03:10,170
due, budget already approved?

54
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:15,270
Be honest with yourself about the answer, because that's exactly the question worth

55
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:19,878
building a habit around rather than trusting willpower to catch every time.

56
00:03:20,428 --> 00:03:24,318
One honest note about where the real risk actually concentrates.

57
00:03:24,318 --> 00:03:29,363
Of the four categories, claims overreach is the one that catches the most drafts in

58
00:03:29,363 --> 00:03:33,132
practice, and it's the one Harlan's account actually ran into.

59
00:03:33,132 --> 00:03:38,116
An early cut of an ad claimed a specific swelling-reduction number nobody had ever

60
00:03:38,116 --> 00:03:38,541
tested.

61
00:03:38,541 --> 00:03:39,575
It never shipped.

62
00:03:39,575 --> 00:03:44,619
But it's exactly the kind of claim that's easy to write without noticing, because a

63
00:03:44,619 --> 00:03:49,543
specific number sounds more credible than a vague one, and credibility is exactly

64
00:03:49,543 --> 00:03:51,488
what a script is trying to earn.

65
00:03:51,488 --> 00:03:53,737
That's the trap worth naming plainly.

66
00:03:53,737 --> 00:03:58,843
Everything Section 2.3's hook taxonomy taught you about specificity, that a concrete

67
00:03:58,843 --> 00:04:03,462
detail beats a vague one, is also exactly what makes an overreached claim so

68
00:04:03,462 --> 00:04:04,556
tempting to write.

69
00:04:04,556 --> 00:04:09,601
The same instinct that makes a good ad good is the instinct that needs the tightest

70
00:04:09,601 --> 00:04:09,966
check.

71
00:04:09,966 --> 00:04:14,707
That's not a coincidence, and it's why claims get their own full lecture next,

72
00:04:14,707 --> 00:04:17,077
rather than a single bullet point here.

73
00:04:17,627 --> 00:04:22,499
Next lecture takes the category that actually caught Harlan's worst draft, and

74
00:04:22,499 --> 00:04:27,308
builds the specific test you can run on any claim before it ships, not just a

75
00:04:27,308 --> 00:04:29,993
general instinct that something sounds off.

76
00:04:29,993 --> 00:04:33,740
Keep the four-question framework close at hand while you go.

77
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:38,487
Each of the next three lectures takes one question and turns it into a real,

78
00:04:38,487 --> 00:04:39,424
specific check.

79
00:04:39,424 --> 00:04:44,670
By Section 6.6, all four come back together as B05, one checklist you run on a whole

80
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:46,856
batch instead of once per question.

81
00:04:46,856 --> 00:04:49,479
Before that, do one honest pass right now.

82
00:04:49,479 --> 00:04:54,662
Pull up whatever you generated in Section 4.6's workshop and run the four questions

83
00:04:54,662 --> 00:04:56,848
against it, out loud if that helps.

84
00:04:56,848 --> 00:05:01,969
You won't have the deep answer to each one yet, this section builds those over the

85
00:05:01,969 --> 00:05:07,153
next three lectures, but you'll already know, roughly, where your own set is strong

86
00:05:07,153 --> 00:05:11,525
and where it's worth paying closer attention as those lectures arrive.
